### 🟦 BASIC CARDS

**Front** | **Back**
--- | ---
What does it mean for vectors \( v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_n \) to be linearly independent? | Only solution to \( a_1v_1 + \ldots + a_nv_n = 0 \) is all \( a_i = 0 \)
What is the trivial linear combination of vectors? | All coefficients zero
What defines linear dependence among vectors \( v_1, \ldots, v_n \)? | Some nonzero combination gives zero vector
If a set includes the zero vector, is it linearly independent? | No
What’s the status of a set containing only \( \vec{0} \)? | Linearly dependent
Is a set with one nonzero vector linearly independent? | Yes
Are two vectors linearly dependent if one is a scalar multiple of the other? | Yes
When are two vectors \( u \) and \( v \) linearly independent? | When they are not parallel
How do you test if vectors are linearly independent? | Solve \( a_1v_1 + \ldots + a_kv_k = 0 \) and check if all \( a_i = 0 \)
What's the key result if the only solution to the homogeneous equation is zero? | Vectors are independent
What must be true for \( \{v_1, \ldots, v_k\} \) to be dependent? | At least one is a linear combo of others
If a vector in a set is a linear combination of the others, the set is...? | Dependent
In \( \mathbb{R}^n \), when is a set \( \{v_1,\ldots,v_n\} \) independent? | When \( Ax = 0 \) has only the trivial solution
Why is a smaller spanning set more efficient? | Fewer vectors, no redundancy
What makes a spanning set “better”? | It is linearly independent
Is every spanning set minimal? | No

